#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e04567-d2e1-5eef-aee3-62d784a9fe49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve Given the First Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "e2790549-46e6-51c2-b118-0c4a056f6d25" :question/math-academy-id "e-599" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x =e^{t}, \\, y= e^{2t}+1$. Given that $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= 2e^t\n$$\n\nand we can calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (e^t) = e^t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(2e^{t}))/(e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2e^{t}}{e^{t}} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "68d38626-1153-51ec-a3c6-d39c927fdd99" :question/math-academy-id "e-600" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\sin(2t), \\qquad y= 2\\cos (2t) \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt \\pi$.\n\nGiven that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -2 \\tan(2t)$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac\\pi 8$." :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}\n$$\n\nwe can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= -2 \\tan(2t)\n$$\n\nand we can calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt} = \\dfrac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt} (\\sin 2t) = 2 \\cos(2t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(-2\\tan 2t))/(2\\cos (2t)) \\\\\n&= (-4\\sec^{2} (2t))/(2\\cos (2t)) \\\\\n&=-4\\sec^{2} (2t) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sec (2t) \\\\\n&=-2\\sec^{3} (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} \\mid _{t = π/8} &= -2\\sec^{3} (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{8}) \\\\\n&=-2\\sec^{3} (\\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&=-2(\\sqrt{2})^{3} \\\\\n&=-4\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068556 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "e621f828-5fc0-55b6-b726-f9da59878574" :question/math-academy-id "e-6803" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=e^t, y=2e^{2t}$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y$, we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=4e^{2t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{4e^{2t}}{e^t} = 4e^t\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dt}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dt}(4e^{t}))/(e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4e^{t}}{e^{t}} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
